        "summary":  "An operator running 49 scheduled AI agents shares lessons learned about real-world failure modes, finding that critical outages stem from infrastructure problems ΓÇö unregistered scheduler jobs, shell portability issues, and absent safety mechanisms ΓÇö rather than from model errors as vendors typically warn about. The article emphasizes monitoring actual system behavior over configuration files, and advocates for agents designed to report unknown status honestly rather than generating confident but incorrect answers. The operational experience contrasts sharply with vendor-provided failure scenario documentation.",
        "execSummary":  "Real-world agent fleet operations are failing at the infrastructure layer ΓÇö schedulers, shell environments, safety mechanisms ΓÇö not at the AI model layer that vendor documentation focuses on, and this practitioner account of 49 production agents is the clearest signal yet of where enterprise monitoring investment should go. For customer-support operations leaders scaling agent deployments, the key takeaway is that standard AI evaluation frameworks miss the actual failure modes; you need infrastructure observability, not just model performance dashboards. The recommendation to build agents that surface unknown rather than hallucinated confidence is directly applicable to support contexts where a wrong answer costs customer trust. Audit your agent monitoring stack against these failure patterns before your next production rollout.",
