        "summary":  "US patent law currently requires human inventors, creating a legal paradox as AI systems like those at Insilico Medicine design novel drug molecules that no individual human specifically conceived. The article examines the DABUS test case and broader industry practices where companies formally list human researchers as inventors despite AI performing core discovery work. Resolving this IP ambiguity will require legislative or judicial action that could reshape incentive structures across AI-driven research industries.",
        "execSummary":  "The AI intellectual property question being litigated in pharmaceutical patents will not stay in pharma ΓÇö any enterprise using AI to generate creative outputs, designs, or solutions faces analogous questions about ownership, liability, and credit. For enterprise leaders, the immediate implication is that AI-generated work product exists in a legal grey zone that current contracts, IP policies, and vendor agreements likely do not address adequately. Organizations that are using AI for product development, content generation, or customer-facing innovation should audit their IP frameworks now before a dispute forces the issue. The caveat is that resolution will be uneven across jurisdictions, requiring multinational enterprises to track legal developments in each operating market.",
